---A widget showing the latest value of a metric, and how this value relates to one or more thresholds.
---@class gcp.unstable.monitoring_v1.Scorecard
---@field sparkChartView? gcp.unstable.monitoring_v1.SparkChartView Will cause the scorecard to show a spark chart.
---@field thresholds? gcp.unstable.monitoring_v1.ThresholdList The thresholds used to determine the state of the scorecard given the time series' current value. For an actual value x, the scorecard is in a danger state if x is less than or equal to a danger threshold that triggers below, or greater than or equal to a danger threshold that triggers above. Similarly, if x is above/below a warning threshold that triggers above/below, then the scorecard is in a warning state - unless x also puts it in a danger state. (Danger trumps warning.)As an example, consider a scorecard with the following four thresholds: { value: 90, category: 'DANGER', trigger: 'ABOVE', }, { value: 70, category: 'WARNING', trigger: 'ABOVE', }, { value: 10, category: 'DANGER', trigger: 'BELOW', }, { value: 20, category: 'WARNING', trigger: 'BELOW', } Then: values less than or equal to 10 would put the scorecard in a DANGER state, values greater than 10 but less than or equal to 20 a WARNING state, values strictly between 20 and 70 an OK state, values greater than or equal to 70 but less than 90 a WARNING state, and values greater than or equal to 90 a DANGER state.
---@field measures? gcp.unstable.monitoring_v1.MeasureList Optional. A measure is a measured value of a property in your data. For example, rainfall in inches, number of units sold, revenue gained, etc.
---@field gaugeView? gcp.unstable.monitoring_v1.GaugeView Will cause the scorecard to show a gauge chart.
---@field blankView? gcp.unstable.monitoring_v1.Empty Will cause the Scorecard to show only the value, with no indicator to its value relative to its thresholds.
---@field timeSeriesQuery? gcp.unstable.monitoring_v1.TimeSeriesQuery Required. Fields for querying time series data from the Stackdriver metrics API.
---@field dimensions? gcp.unstable.monitoring_v1.DimensionList Optional. A dimension is a structured label, class, or category for a set of measurements in your data.
---@field breakdowns? gcp.unstable.monitoring_v1.BreakdownList Optional. The collection of breakdowns to be applied to the dataset. A breakdown is a way to slice the data. For example, you can break down the data by region.

